# /etc/kimi-k3.env — read by kimi-k3.service (systemd EnvironmentFile).
#
# CRITICAL: systemd EnvironmentFile does NOT strip inline '# ...' comments — it folds the rest of
# the line into the value. Keep every KEY=value BARE; put comments on their own full lines only.
#
# Switch quant/build source = edit this file, then: sudo systemctl restart kimi-k3

# --- checkpoint + build ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# QUANT: any subdirectory of the unsloth/Kimi-K3-GGUF snapshot. Verified end-to-end (SKILL.md step 2):
#   UD-Q2_K_XL          802 GiB  hybrid (~130 GiB of experts on CPU)   6.4 tok/s c1, best quality
#   UD-IQ2_XXS          662 GiB  all-VRAM                              13.9 tok/s c1, KLD 0.37 vs Q2_K_XL
#   UD-Q2_K_XL-REAP770  697 GiB  all-VRAM, locally sliced (REFERENCE.md "REAP expert slicing")
#                                                                       8.3 tok/s c1, KLD 0.047
QUANT=UD-Q2_K_XL-REAP770
# BUILD_SOURCE: fork (vision, unmerged unslothai/llama.cpp PR #70) | mainline (text-only)
BUILD_SOURCE=fork
# IMAGE: pin it — several images coexist once you have rebuilt (…-lip = with the K3 layer-input patch
# and llama-perplexity/llama-imatrix). Unset = newest kimi-k3-llamacpp:${BUILD_SOURCE}-* on this host.
IMAGE=kimi-k3-llamacpp:fork-883f2c9ba78f-lip-rsrb

# --- host / paths ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute HF_HOME — your HF hub cache root (systemd does NOT expand ~ or $HOME). EDIT THIS.
HF_HOME=/path/to/hf-cache
PORT=30000
NAME=kimi-k3
MODEL_NAME=kimi-k3

# --- CPU/GPU expert split ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset = let llama.cpp's --fit choose -ngl and the GPU/CPU expert split from live free VRAM.
# That is the verified default: it lands ~92-94 GB on each of 8 GPUs and answers a first request
# in ~3 s. Do NOT go back to the old --cpu-moe default (all experts CPU-resident) — measured
# 2026-09-03, that made a single 32-token request run 20+ min and never finish.
# Set N_CPU_MOE to pin exactly N layers' experts to CPU instead of --fit's live choice.
# N_CPU_MOE=20

# --- concurrency -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CTX_SIZE is the TOTAL context across all slots, not per-slot: CTX_SIZE/PARALLEL = per-request
# context. Raising PARALLEL at fixed CTX_SIZE costs NO extra KV memory (it only re-partitions),
# so there is no VRAM reason to leave it at 1 — and at PARALLEL=1 every concurrent client just
# queues behind a single slot. 65536/16 = 4096 tokens per request; raise CTX_SIZE proportionally
# for longer contexts.
#
# PARALLEL is MEASURED per quant (llm-inference-benchmark skill, Kimi-K3 section): hybrid Q2_K_XL
# with -ub 4096 peaks at c8 (16.0 tok/s); all-VRAM IQ2_XXS peaks at c4-c8 (~25 tok/s) and regresses
# above; all-VRAM REAP-770 (fit 4096) reaches 21.9 tok/s at c8 and is flat beyond (8-slot cap).
# TTFT past c8 is pure prompt queueing for every quant, so 8 slots x 8192 tokens is the default.
CTX_SIZE=65536
PARALLEL=8
# BATCH/UBATCH (-b/-ub). HYBRID quant: 4096 — llama.cpp re-copies every CPU-resident expert tensor
# (~100 GiB) to GPU0 for EACH micro-batch >= 32 tokens, so one micro-batch per prompt instead of two
# cut TTFT 34-49 % (measured). ALL-VRAM quant: 2048 — nothing to amortise, and 4096 plus the default
# 1 GiB fit margin OOMs the CUDA scratch pool on the first request (measured).
BATCH=2048
UBATCH=2048
# EXTRA_ARGS: free-form llama-server flags. ALL-VRAM quants REQUIRE a --fit-target margin above the
# default 1024 MiB (CUDA scratch pool) — but not too large: REAP-770 (697 GiB) needs 4096 to stay
# all-GPU (6144 silently spilled ~20 GiB of experts to the CPU and cost 40 % of decode); IQ2_XXS is
# fine at 6144-8192. Per-device list form: --fit-target 5120,5120,5120,5120,5120,5120,5120,13312.
# Other uses: "--spec-type ngram-mod" (≈ neutral, REFERENCE.md), "--fit off -ngl 999 --n-cpu-moe N",
# DSpark (single-stream / c<=2 only; needs an image with BOTH patches, e.g. IMAGE=...-lip-rsrb):
#   "-md /models/repo/drafts/Kimi-K3-DSpark-bf16.gguf --spec-type draft-dspark --spec-draft-n-max 7 -ngld 99
#    --spec-draft-device CUDA7 --fit-target 5632,5632,5632,5632,5632,5632,5632,10240" — the draft MUST sit on the
#   GPU holding the target's output.weight (the last one) with ~9 GB free there, and the rollback snapshots
#   need ~14 GB spread over the GPUs at 4 slots (REFERENCE.md "Speculative decoding"). +23 % at c1, loses at c4. Quote the value (systemd EnvironmentFile keeps quoted values with spaces intact).
EXTRA_ARGS="--fit-target 4096"
# DOCKER_ENV: extra `docker run -e KEY=VAL` entries for A/Bs, e.g. "GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1".
# DOCKER_ENV=

# --- GPU selection -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset = all 8 GPUs (--device nvidia.com/gpu=all). Override to a subset if this host's GPU pool
# is shared and not fully free — check `nvidia-smi` before claiming any (AGENTS.md convention).
# GPU_ARGS=--device nvidia.com/gpu=4 --device nvidia.com/gpu=5 --device nvidia.com/gpu=6 --device nvidia.com/gpu=7

# --- other -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOST — bind address. serve_docker.sh defaults to this host's Tailscale IP (`tailscale ip -4`),
# falling back to loopback-only if Tailscale is down — never a silent 0.0.0.0, which would also
# expose the raw port on the LAN and the Docker/LXD bridges. Set explicitly only to override that.
# See the deploy directory's own README for the access model chosen for a given deployment.
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# JINJA — needed for tool-call parsing; default on (1). Set 0 to disable.
# JINJA=1
